---
import ProjectPost from "../../../components/ProjectPost.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import cover from "./20241006-1.png";

export const meta = {
    title: "Rebuilding awesome-prometheus-alerts in Astro",
    date: new Date(2024, 9, 6),
    description: "A take-home assignment for a job I was interviewing for",
    tech: ["astro", "react"],
    role: ["Engineering"],
    image: cover,
};
---

<ProjectPost meta={meta}>
    <p>
        I was interviewing for a front-end engineer gig with a startup using
        React and Astro for their marketing projects that I would work on. As an
        assignment, they wanted me to recreate <a
            href="https://github.com/samber">samber</a
        >'s <a href="https://github.com/samber/awesome-prometheus-alerts"
            >awesome-prometheus-alerts</a
        > with a new design. The <a
            href="https://github.com/azan-n/prometheus-alerts"
            ><strong>source code</strong></a
        > for the rewritten website is up on GitHub. The website is hosted at <a
            href="https://awesome-prometheus.azan-n.com"
            >awesome-prometheus.azan-n.com</a
        >
    </p><p>
        I started the project by studying the Jekyll based <a
            href="https://github.com/samber/awesome-prometheus-alerts"
            >awesome-prometheus-alerts</a
        > project to figure out how it was using the YAML data from <a
            href="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/samber/awesome-prometheus-alerts/refs/heads/master/_data/rules.yml"
            >rules.yml</a
        > to JSON using <code>yq</code>, and eventually through a set of
        templates, generating HTML files.
    </p><p>
        I started by using <a
            href="https://bundlephobia.com/package/js-yaml@4.1.0">js-yaml</a
        > to fetch the data source <code>rules.yml</code> and convert it to JSON on
        build time. I made the following observations:
    </p><ol>
        <li>
            It might be a good idea to convert the <code>rules.yml</code> to JSON
            and store it in the git repository instead of fetching it every time we
            build, and re-fetch periodically since it is the only variable data source.
            (The original project does the same in the <a
                href="https://github.com/samber/awesome-prometheus-alerts/blob/35596c866f129e3134f7ac705e90f50002dae073/.github/workflows/dist.yml#L32"
                >build step</a
            >, <em
                >I wonder why they store the default in YAML if they end up
                converting it to JSON anyway</em
            >)
        </li><li>
            <a href="https://simpleicons.org/">Simple Icons</a> when used with <a
                href="https://www.astroicon.dev/getting-started/">astro-icon</a
            > has no color by default, which isn't an acceptable state.
        </li><li>
            I never noticed this before but Astro components and <code
                >&lt;script&gt;</code
            > tags within them cannot share variables directly which is a bit of a
            pain.
        </li><li>
            I ran into <a href="https://github.com/npm/cli/issues/4828"
                >npm issues</a
            > with <code>package-lock.json</code> not handling OS-specific dependencies
            correctly. Using pnpm fixed the <code>package-lock.json</code> issue,
            not sure how.
        </li>
    </ol><h2>Async flashbacks</h2><p>
        I hadn't explored the React integration with Astro in a while and wanted
        to see if I could borrow some React components into the project.
        However, as of 10/06/2024, React in Astro is like async in JS, you make
        one function async and everything ends up being async. You make one
        component JSX/TSX and almost everything ends up being JSX/TSX.
    </p><p>
        With the React integration, the architecture of how to use Astro
        components and React components together has to be made carefully since <a
            href="https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/framework-components/#can-i-use-astro-components-inside-my-framework-components"
            >framework components</a
        > cannot use Astro components within them. I wanted syntax-highlighting to
        be done using <a href="https://shiki.matsu.io/">Shiki</a> on build-time because
        the code being highlighted does not change on the client. I was able to make
        this work by passing a statically rendered Astro component as a child to a
        React dialog. The <a
            href="https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/integrations-guide/react/#children-parsing"
            ><code>experimentalReactChildren: true</code></a
        > property seems promising but it is rightly marked as experimental.
    </p>
    <h2>Searching</h2>
    <p>
        Using React for handling the searching would, in the possibilities that
        I explored, make the Astro project a regular React SPA, with the pain
        points of the <code>@astro/react</code> integration. To prevent the same,
        I implemented searching using vanilla JS and HTML data attributes.
    </p>

    <h2>Looking ahead</h2>
    <figure>
        <Image class="w-full" src={cover} alt="" />
        <figcaption>
            The lighthouse score is pretty good for something that uses React,
            and was built in a day or so.
        </figcaption>
    </figure><p>
        JSX is great, it is expressive, components are functions and functions
        can be passed around and rendered, this allows for flexible composition,
        which I miss when using Astro components. I look forward to a time where
        I can do actual SSG using React components.
    </p>
</ProjectPost>
